    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nOwner deletion is now a soft-delete. The changes, kept small and cohesive to minimize structural impact:\n\n**Domain / persistence**\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added a `deleted` boolean column plus one-line `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()` accessors.\n- `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` to the `owners` table (this is the profile the tests use; `ddl-auto=none`).\n- `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 appended `FALSE` to the 10 seed owner rows (positional inserts). The h2 profile auto-adds the column via `ddl-auto=update`; postgres/mysql schemas are already stale relative to the entity and untested, so I left them untouched.\n\n**API contract**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field and MapStruct auto-maps it. GET responses now carry `deleted`.\n\n**Behavior**\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.deleteOwner` \u2014 instead of removing the row, it sets `deleted = true` and saves, still returning 2xx.\n- `OwnerUniqueIdentityAdvice` \u2014 both create-side checks now skip soft-deleted owners: the identity-key loop gained a `!existing.isDeleted()` guard, and the household check filters deleted owners out before `Households.isDuplicate`. No new methods; each is a single-line change in the same file.\n\nVerified:\n- A new owner reports `deleted: false`; after DELETE, GET still returns the owner with `deleted: true`.\n- A normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only matching owner has been soft-deleted.\n\n`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS`.",
